{"content":"[P2] Reference error text that the accessibility API exposes. The selector points directly to the status element, but its accessible description is empty. A screen-reader user who returns to the selector does not receive the calendar error through that description.","codeSuggestion":"","assessment":"Confirmed independently in two existing test runs and a headless check of the production dialog.","rationale":"The new test times out at browser_calendarEventCreateSave.js:377. The DOM relationship resolves, but getAccessibleFor(select).description returns an empty string. A plain span exposes its text. A child span inside the status element also exposes the complete error while the outer element retains role=status.","recommendation":"change","validation":"Both create Save runs failed at the description check. The production check reproduced the empty description. The child-span check returned the exact localized error. The later calendar-validation failures follow from the first test exception leaving calendar.readOnly true.","appliedSummary":"Updated the renderer, production template, browser fixture, and both Save tests. The selector now references localized child text. The outer polite status remains.

Syntax, XML, whitespace, and JavaScript formatting checks passed. Edited XHTML blocks match Prettier; unrelated existing formatting differences remain. The diff follows the recorded patterns.

Runtime tests were not run because this task forbids generated files outside `comm`. The evidence assessment remains incomplete. All five files remain unstaged.","changeSummary":"Put localized Save text in a child of the status element. Reference that child from the selector. Retain the outer polite status role and the current description, retention, and retry assertions. Update the production and fixture templates.","state":"ready","changeAccepted":true,"changeReverted":false,"changesAmended":false}
